 ### [Parallel Cell Projection](875-parallel-cell-projection.md)
-Parallel Cell Projection is a distributed-memory strategy for object-order volume rendering that decomposes the workload into an object-space projection phase and an image-space composition phase. Unlike ray-casting, it avoids the need for global depth-sorting of cells by having each processor scan-convert its local unstructured cells into ray segments, which are then routed to their final image destinations for merging. It sets itself apart from synchronous cell projection by allowing asynchronous communication and task management to hide data transfer overheads.
+Parallel Cell Projection is a parallelization strategy for Cell-Projection Volume Rendering that distributes the computationally expensive task of software-based polygon generation and the subsequent image-space composition across multiple processors or threads. It sets itself apart from synchronous alternatives by employing asynchronous communication and task management to hide data transfer overheads, and from concurrent scan-conversion/composition approaches by strictly separating these phases to reduce inter-processor communication frequency. It is chosen when rendering large unstructured grids on distributed-memory systems to balance load and scale efficiently.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
 *   **Time Complexity:** The algorithm avoids offline preprocessing, performing low-cost preprocessing (e.g., view-dependent optimization) in parallel [S2, unofficial]. The rendering time is dominated by scan conversion and communication [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage is proportional to the local data size and the number of ray segments buffered for asynchronous communication [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage is proportional to the local data size and the number of ray segments buffered for asynchronous communication [S1, S2, unofficial].
 *   **Measured Performance:**
     *   Rendering 0.5 million tetrahedral cells into a 400x400 pixel image takes 0.5 seconds (2 frames per second) on 128 IBM SP2 nodes (excluding display time) [S1, unofficial].
